  "scope": "The ORIGINAL retained carrier now has both signed bounds after combining a separated family of complete prime periods BEFORE taking the quadratic mean. At any divisor cutoff at most one interval is partially active. The joint signed energy retains the total full moment squared and pays only one maximum partial-tail amplitude. Actual factorial periods have numerical C_ij=4W_ij/a_i^2+W_ij*s_ij*h*B_i and B_ij=W_ij*(1+s_ij*h)*B_i, with all prime-error and amplitude premises discharged for a_i>=5000 and abs(y)>=54. The explicit joint energy is lo*(sum C_ij)^2+(hi-lo)*(sum C_ij+max B_ij)^2. A proved but UNEVALUATED E>0 gives squarefree mean E*X*Q_j with the full cofactor phase retained across the entire family. Reciprocal cofactor energy is paid. The terminal original residualCoefficient*zetaPrimeLogKernel sum keeps boundedShare, both Riesz hinges, every factorial order including zero and one, and all squarefree counts from three upward. Its numerical cost is sqrt(E)/(L*N!)*exp(-log M/2)*sum_j choose(N+1,j)*log(2M)^(N+1-j)*sqrt(Q_j), rather than a sum of period norms. The selected cofactor population must be COMMON to every combined period, which must be complete, eligible and coprime to every selected cofactor. Cofactor-dependent prime holes and clipped boundaries remain UNPAID. This is an alternative bound, not a uniform claim of improvement over the previous budget. Optional numerical tests show gains on central blocks but do not prove source-scale decay. The combined moment cost and total source-normalized aggregation remain OPEN. No zero hypothesis, simplicity assumption, generic prime-density transfer, low-order deletion or duplicate paid credit is used. Both whole -79/1000 floor and 3/2 ceiling remain open; no zero exclusion or RH claim.",
